\begin{abstract}
The Riccati equation $v' + v^2 + p\,v + q = 0$ is the simplest genuinely nonlinear
ordinary differential equation and a hinge between linear and nonlinear theory.
It is classical that a single known solution $v_0$ reduces it to a first-order
linear equation via the substitution $v = v_0 + 1/u$. We give a fully rigorous,
purely algebraic treatment of this reduction in an \emph{arbitrary differential
field} $(K, {}')$---no analytic, topological, characteristic, or algebraic-closure
hypotheses are used. Our central result is a single \emph{cleared} algebraic identity,
\[
\big[(v_0 + u^{-1})' + (v_0 + u^{-1})^2 + p(v_0 + u^{-1}) + q\big]\,u^2
   = (2v_0 + p)\,u + 1 - u',
\]
from which we derive, as an exact equivalence, that $v = v_0 + 1/u$ solves the
Riccati equation \emph{iff} $u$ solves the affine linear equation
$u' = (2v_0 + p)\,u + 1$. We prove the converse extraction map $u = 1/(v - v_0)$,
establishing a bijection between Riccati solutions distinct from $v_0$ and solutions
of the linear equation, and we record the Bernoulli companion identity
$(v - v_0)'/(v - v_0) = -(v + v_0 + p)$. We then place these facts inside
differential Galois theory: the general Riccati equation carries a projective
$\PGL_2(\text{constants})$ symmetry (witnessed by the constancy of the cross-ratio of
four solutions), and fixing one solution restricts this symmetry to the
\emph{solvable affine stabilizer} $\Ga \rtimes \Gm$. This is the structural reason a
Riccati equation with one known solution is integrable by quadratures. All results
are machine-verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The Riccati equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:R}
v' + v^2 + p\,v + q = 0 \tag{R}
\end{equation}
occupies a privileged place in the theory of differential equations. It is first
order and quadratic---the minimal departure from linearity---yet it controls a
remarkable range of phenomena: the logarithmic derivative $v = y'/y$ of a
second-order linear equation satisfies a Riccati equation, so~\eqref{eq:R} is the
nonlinear avatar of all second-order linear theory. In applications it governs
linear-quadratic optimal control (the matrix Riccati equation), the WKB/eikonal
expansion in semiclassical analysis, supersymmetric quantum mechanics, and
eigenvalue flows.

The equation~\eqref{eq:R} has two faces. \textbf{Negatively}, its solutions can be
transcendental: the Airy Riccati equation $v' + v^2 = x$ admits no rational
solution. \textbf{Positively}, there is a classical reduction principle: \emph{one
known solution linearizes the equation}. If $v_0$ solves~\eqref{eq:R}, the
substitution $v = v_0 + 1/u$ removes the nonlinearity and produces a first-order
linear equation for $u$, solvable by an integrating factor.

This paper gives a complete, elementary, and fully general proof of the positive
reduction, and frames it within differential Galois theory. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A \textbf{division-free cleared identity} (Theorem~\ref{thm:identity}), the
        algebraic engine of the linearization, valid for any $u \neq 0$.
  \item The \textbf{linearization equivalence} (Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}): an exact
        ``iff'' between solvability of~\eqref{eq:R} by $v_0 + 1/u$ and the affine
        linear equation for $u$.
  \item The \textbf{converse extraction} (Theorem~\ref{thm:converse}): any second
        solution $v$ yields $u = 1/(v - v_0)$ solving the linear equation---a
        bijection.
  \item The \textbf{Bernoulli companion} (Theorem~\ref{thm:bernoulli}): the
        logarithmic derivative of the gap $v - v_0$.
  \item A \textbf{differential-Galois synthesis} (Section~\ref{sec:galois}): the
        projective $\PGL_2$ symmetry of the general equation and its restriction to
        the solvable affine stabilizer when $v_0$ is fixed.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting and definitions}

\subsection{Differential fields}

\begin{definition}[Differential field]
A \emph{differential field} is a field $K$ equipped with a \emph{derivation}
${}' : K \to K$---an additive map satisfying the Leibniz rule
\[ (xy)' = x'\,y + x\,y' \qquad (x, y \in K). \]
An element $c \in K$ with $c' = 0$ is a \emph{constant}; the constants form a
subfield $C \subseteq K$.
\end{definition}

From the axioms one derives the quotient rule $(x/y)' = (x'y - xy')/y^2$, the
reciprocal rule $(y^{-1})' = -y^{-2}y'$, and the power rule
$(y^n)' = n\,y^{n-1}y'$ for $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, $y \neq 0$. No assumption is made
on the characteristic of $K$, on algebraic closure, or on the size of $C$. The
canonical model is $K = \mathbb{C}(x)$ with $' = d/dx$ and $C = \mathbb{C}$.

\subsection{The logarithmic derivative}

\begin{definition}[Logarithmic derivative]
For $y \in K^\times$, the \emph{logarithmic derivative} is $\Lder(y) := y'/y$.
\end{definition}

The Leibniz rule makes $\Lder$ a homomorphism from $(K^\times, \cdot)$ to $(K, +)$:
\[ \Lder(yz) = \Lder(y) + \Lder(z), \quad \Lder(y/z) = \Lder(y) - \Lder(z),
   \quad \Lder(y^n) = n\,\Lder(y). \]
Its kernel is the nonzero constants; this is why first-order linear equations
exponentiate.

\subsection{First-order linear equations and their symmetry}

\begin{definition}[First-order linear / affine equations]
For $a, b \in K$: the \emph{homogeneous} equation is $w' = a\,w$; the \emph{affine}
equation is $u' = a\,u + b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Multiplicative structure]\label{prop:ratio}
If $y_1' = a\,y_1$ and $y_2' = a\,y_2$ with $y_1 \neq 0$, then $(y_2/y_1)' = 0$;
consequently any two nonzero solutions of $w' = a\,w$ differ by a nonzero constant
factor, and the nonzero solutions form a $\Gm(C)$-torsor.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By the quotient rule, $(y_2/y_1)' = (y_2'y_1 - y_2 y_1')/y_1^2
 = (a y_2 y_1 - a y_2 y_1)/y_1^2 = 0$.
\end{proof}

This is the rank-1 Picard--Vessiot statement: the differential Galois group of
$w' = a\,w$ is a subgroup of $\Gm(C)$.

\subsection{The Riccati equation and the cross-ratio}

We call $v \in K$ a \emph{Riccati solution} (for fixed $p, q$) if it
satisfies~\eqref{eq:R}.

\begin{definition}[Cross-ratio]
For $v_1, v_2, v_3, v_4 \in K$ with $v_1 \neq v_4$, $v_2 \neq v_3$,
\[ [\,v_1, v_2; v_3, v_4\,] :=
   \frac{(v_1 - v_3)(v_2 - v_4)}{(v_1 - v_4)(v_2 - v_3)}. \]
\end{definition}

\section{The linearization theorems}

Fix $p, q \in K$ and a Riccati solution $v_0$, i.e.\ $v_0' + v_0^2 + p\,v_0 + q = 0$.

\subsection{The cleared identity}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{riccati\_oneSolution\_identity}]\label{thm:identity}
For every $u \in K$ with $u \neq 0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:identity}
\big[(v_0 + u^{-1})' + (v_0 + u^{-1})^2 + p\,(v_0 + u^{-1}) + q\big]\cdot u^2
   = (2v_0 + p)\,u + 1 - u'.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Using additivity of $'$ and the reciprocal rule $(u^{-1})' = -u^{-2}u'$,
$(v_0 + u^{-1})' = v_0' - u^{-2}u'$. Expanding $(v_0 + u^{-1})^2 = v_0^2 + 2v_0
u^{-1} + u^{-2}$ and $p(v_0 + u^{-1}) = pv_0 + pu^{-1}$, the bracket becomes
\[
\underbrace{(v_0' + v_0^2 + pv_0 + q)}_{= 0 \text{ by }\eqref{eq:R}}
 + (2v_0 + p)u^{-1} + u^{-2} - u^{-2}u'.
\]
The first group vanishes since $v_0$ solves~\eqref{eq:R}. Multiplying the survivors
by $u^2$ gives $(2v_0 + p)u + 1 - u'$. Formally, after clearing denominators, the
identity is the linear combination $u^2 \cdot (\text{R at } v_0)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The linearization equivalence}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{riccati\_solvable\_iff\_linear}]\label{thm:iff}
Let $u \neq 0$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:iff}
(v_0 + u^{-1})' + (v_0 + u^{-1})^2 + p\,(v_0 + u^{-1}) + q = 0
 \iff u' = (2v_0 + p)\,u + 1.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $R(u)$ be the Riccati expression at $v = v_0 + u^{-1}$ and
$S(u) := (2v_0 + p)u + 1 - u'$. Theorem~\ref{thm:identity} states
$R(u)\,u^2 = S(u)$. If $R(u) = 0$ then $S(u) = 0$. Conversely if $S(u) = 0$ then
$R(u)\,u^2 = 0$, and $u^2 \neq 0$ forces $R(u) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Geometric meaning of the coefficient]
The Riccati vector field $v \mapsto -v^2 - pv - q$ has derivative $-2v - p$, equal
to $-(2v_0 + p)$ at $v_0$. Thus the coefficient $2v_0 + p$ is the Jacobian of the
Riccati field at the known solution; the ``$+1$'' is the translation term created by
the reciprocal substitution.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Converse extraction and the bijection}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{riccati\_solution\_gives\_linear}]\label{thm:converse}
If $v$ is a Riccati solution with $v \neq v_0$, then $u := (v - v_0)^{-1}$ satisfies
\[ u' = (2v_0 + p)\,u + 1. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $v \neq v_0$, $d := v - v_0 \neq 0$ and $u = d^{-1} \neq 0$. Then
$v_0 + u^{-1} = v_0 + d = v$, so the Riccati expression at $v_0 + u^{-1}$ equals that
at $v$, which is $0$. Apply the forward direction of Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bijection of solution sets]
The maps $u \mapsto v_0 + u^{-1}$ and $v \mapsto (v - v_0)^{-1}$ are mutually inverse
bijections between $\{u \neq 0 : u' = (2v_0 + p)u + 1\}$ and
$\{v \neq v_0 : v \text{ solves }\eqref{eq:R}\}$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The Bernoulli companion}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{riccati\_secondSolution\_diff\_logDeriv}]\label{thm:bernoulli}
If $v$ and $v_0$ both solve~\eqref{eq:R} and $v \neq v_0$, then
\[ \frac{(v - v_0)'}{v - v_0} = -(v + v_0 + p). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Subtracting the Riccati equations, $(v - v_0)' = -(v^2 - v_0^2) - p(v - v_0)
 = -(v + v_0 + p)(v - v_0)$; divide by $v - v_0 \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Differential-Galois synthesis: from projective to affine}\label{sec:galois}

\subsection{The general equation is projective}

\begin{theorem}[Difference law; \texttt{riccati\_diff}]\label{thm:diff}
If $v_1, v_2$ both solve~\eqref{eq:R}, then
$(v_1 - v_2)' = -(v_1 + v_2 + p)(v_1 - v_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Cross-ratio is constant; \texttt{riccati\_crossRatio\_isConstant}]
\label{thm:crossratio}
If $v_1, v_2, v_3, v_4$ all solve~\eqref{eq:R}, with $v_1 \neq v_4$ and
$v_2 \neq v_3$, then $[\,v_1, v_2; v_3, v_4\,]' = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:diff} each factor $v_i - v_j$ solves a homogeneous first-order
equation with coefficient $-(v_i + v_j + p)$. By the homomorphism property of
$\Lder$, the numerator and denominator of the cross-ratio solve equations with
coefficients $-(v_1+v_3+p)-(v_2+v_4+p)$ and $-(v_1+v_4+p)-(v_2+v_3+p)$; their
quotient solves $w' = \kappa w$ with
\[ \kappa = \big[-(v_1{+}v_3{+}p)-(v_2{+}v_4{+}p)\big]
          - \big[-(v_1{+}v_4{+}p)-(v_2{+}v_3{+}p)\big] = 0, \]
the terms telescoping. Hence the cross-ratio has zero derivative.
\end{proof}

Constancy of the cross-ratio says the differential Galois group of~\eqref{eq:R}
acts by Möbius transformations: it is a subgroup of $\PGL_2(C)$, and the solution
set is a single $\PGL_2(C)$-orbit.

\subsection{One solution restricts to the solvable affine group}

In $\PGL_2$ the stabilizer of a point is the affine group
$\Aff_1 = \Ga \rtimes \Gm$ of maps $w \mapsto \alpha w + \beta$. Fixing $v_0$ passes
to this stabilizer, realized concretely by Theorems~\ref{thm:identity}--\ref{thm:converse}:
the reciprocal coordinate $u = 1/(v - v_0)$ converts the point-fixing projective
action into the affine action on $u$; the homogeneous part $u' = (2v_0+p)u$ is the
scaling ($\Gm$) part with a $\Gm(C)$-line of solutions
(Proposition~\ref{prop:ratio}); the ``$+1$'' is the translation ($\Ga$) part.

If $v_1, v_2 \neq v_0$ are solutions, then $u_i = 1/(v_i - v_0)$ each solve the
affine equation, and the difference $u_1 - u_2$ solves the homogeneous equation
$w' = (2v_0+p)w$ (the inhomogeneous terms cancel); two such differences have
constant ratio. Because $\Aff_1 = \Ga \rtimes \Gm$ is \emph{solvable}, the equation
it governs is integrable by quadratures.

\begin{quote}
A single known solution of a Riccati equation restricts its differential Galois
group from the projective $\PGL_2(C)$ to the solvable affine stabilizer
$\Ga \rtimes \Gm(C)$, which is exactly why the equation becomes integrable.
\end{quote}

\subsection{The negative boundary}

The reduction is vacuous if no solution exists in $K$. The Airy Riccati equation
$v' + v^2 = x$ over $\mathbb{C}(x)$ has no rational solution: there is no $v_0$ to
fix, the Galois group never descends below the projective level, and the solutions
are transcendental. The positive results here are the mirror of that obstruction.

\section{Algorithmic content}

\textbf{Algorithm (Riccati reduction by a known solution).}
\emph{Input:} $p, q \in K$ and a solution $v_0$.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Form the affine equation $u' = (2v_0 + p)u + 1$.
  \item Solve the homogeneous part by one quadrature:
        $w = \exp\!\int (2v_0 + p)$.
  \item Find a particular solution by variation of constants:
        $u_p = w \int w^{-1}$.
  \item The general solution is $u = u_p + C\,w$.
  \item Output $v = v_0 + 1/u$.
\end{enumerate}
This captures every solution $v \neq v_0$; $v = v_0$ is the limit $C \to \infty$.
The cost is two quadratures. Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} doubles as a cheap,
division-free correctness certificate for a claimed solution.

\section{Applications}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Control theory:} the scalar LQR reduces to a Riccati equation; a
        known equilibrium linearizes the transient analysis.
  \item \textbf{SUSY quantum mechanics:} with $v = \psi'/\psi$, a known ground state
        supplies $v_0$; the linearization is the core of Darboux/factorization
        methods.
  \item \textbf{Special functions:} connection formulas for second-order linear ODEs
        descend to the constant cross-ratio of Theorem~\ref{thm:crossratio}.
  \item \textbf{Certified solving:} Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} gives a polynomial-identity
        certificate for symbolic Riccati solvers.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and future work}

The results crystallize a trichotomy for the Riccati equation: projective
$\PGL_2(C)$ symmetry in general, affine/solvable collapse once one solution is
known, and a negative boundary when no solution exists. Future directions include:
(1) the second-order linearization (reduction of order) via $v = y'/y$;
(2) the matrix Riccati equation of optimal control, with $\PGL_2$ replaced by a
Grassmannian action; (3) a certificate-driven Kovacic-style classification of
closed-form solvability; and (4) the full rank-2 Picard--Vessiot correspondence.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a complete, fully general, machine-verified proof that one known
solution linearizes the Riccati equation, organized around a single cleared
algebraic identity. The linearization is an exact equivalence, the second-solution
map is a bijection, and the phenomenon is the restriction of a projective
$\PGL_2(C)$ symmetry to its solvable affine stabilizer $\Ga \rtimes \Gm(C)$---the
differential-Galois reason a Riccati equation with a known solution is integrable by
quadratures.

